# Brazen - fourteen instruments, and they are meant to be telling apart.
#
# The bank this replaces had nine patches whose spectral centroids were 2427,
# 2423 and 2422 Hz for Trombone, Trumpet and Growl - three names on one sound,
# and seven of the nine between 2420 and 2800. Dan heard it before the harness
# was asked the right question.
#
# The right question, for this machine, is the harmonic ladder and not the
# centroid, and Brazen's own section of the plan says so: "the centroid moves
# only 614 to 645 Hz, because the fundamental grows with everything else; the
# ladder is the evidence". `audition play Brazen Trumpet --ladder` prints it.
# A tuba is a fundamental with two partials over it; a trumpet is an even
# rolloff eight harmonics deep. That is the difference, and it is measurable.
#
# Three things move it, and the old bank varied none of them enough:
#
#   size      the bore, 0 a tuba's wide slow one and 1 a trumpet's narrow one
#   bell      how much of the wave comes back - and it is not a brightness
#             slider. The ladder peaks around 0.6 and falls away on both
#             sides: too open and nothing reflects to build a standing wave,
#             too closed and the top of it is absorbed. The first draft of
#             this bank put every bright instrument at 0.14 to 0.35, off the
#             far edge, and made them all thin instead of bright
#   brass     the shock front - and it only bites in the upper register, which
#             is why a bank played at one pitch could not hear it at all
#
# And the fourth is register. These do not transpose: a preset that silently
# moves the keyboard is a preset that surprises you later. Each says the note
# it is meant for instead, which is where `audition bank Brazen` plays it.
#
# One thing not to touch: `tension` is not a tone control. It is how tight the
# lips are, and on a brass instrument that decides *which partial of the tube
# you are playing* - which is how a bugle works. Moved off 1 it detunes:
# measured at F4, tension 0.7 plays 97 cents sharp, 1.2 plays 55 flat and 1.35
# plays 997 cents sharp, and the harmonic ladder collapses from twelve
# harmonics to three or four every time, because the lips are fighting the
# tube instead of driving it. Three patches here had it set for brightness and
# all three were playing the wrong note. Use it to pick a partial deliberately
# or leave it alone.
#
# The other thing that had to be voiced, and could not be seen at all until
# there was a column for it, is **how long a patch takes to speak**. A
# waveguide builds a standing wave over hundreds of milliseconds at low
# pitches, so a big instrument can be perfectly good on a held note and
# produce almost nothing in a phrase. Measured against the level the note
# settles at, the first draft read: Tuba 740 ms, Bass Trombone 550, Horn 520,
# Trombone 310 - against notes 475 ms long. Three of them never reached half
# volume before they were released, and what you heard was a burst of
# excitation with no tube behind it, which is the "clicky, missing notes" Dan
# reported. A shorter attack, more pressure and more bite bring them all
# under 210 ms, and the ladder holds its gradient.
#
# The machine's own defaults still take 480 ms, which is worth knowing before
# anybody writes a low patch from Init.
#
# **The click on the front of a note** was the third thing that had to be
# voiced and the third that no existing number could see. It is not an
# overshoot - these measured well *under* the tone they settle into - it is a
# burst of high frequency the body of the sound never has, and the darker the
# instrument the more it sticks out.
#
# **And it was the machine's, not the bank's.** The right way to see it is the
# first pass down the tube: at note-on the line is empty, so nothing comes out
# of the bell until the wave has been down and back - 11.7 ms on this tuba,
# 5.7 on the horn - and what arrives then has been round the loop *once*. The
# settled tone has been round it hundreds of times. Measured as the ratio
# between the two above 1.5 kHz, the machine read Tuba 24.7x, Horn 24.1x,
# Trombone 11.5x and Trumpet 0.9x - which is Dan's complaint list in order,
# and the reason the trumpet was never on it.
#
# Four things were wrong and all four are fixed in the machine:
#
#   - the mouth pressure was worked out once per 64-frame block and held, so
#     a 50 ms attack went into the tube as 37 treads of 4% each - a 750 Hz
#     sawtooth on the front of every note
#   - the line length is solved once a block too, and it moves while a note
#     starts, so the read pointer walked about seventeen samples in a hundred
#     discrete jumps
#   - the lip follower was one symmetric pole at a fixed 34.7 ms. A one-pole
#     chasing an input that is still growing settles at a fraction of it, so
#     the valve spent every onset some three times further open than the note
#     it was playing. It counts in cycles now, three up and twelve down
#   - and the hiss was flat to Nyquist, which no airstream is
#
# It now reads Tuba 2.1x, Horn 4.6x, Trombone 4.5x, Trumpet 0.2x, and the
# onset edge column across the whole bank is 2.4x at worst against 5.3x.
#
# **So the low-pass came off.** It was chosen to tame that onset and says so
# above; the onset is gone at the source, and the filter was costing the dark
# instruments their body for nothing. Tuba 850 Hz to 1600, Bass Trombone 2000
# to 3200, Trombone 3000 to 5000, Horn 3400 to 4800, Cup Mute 2400 to 3200.
# What is left is chosen for tone, which is its job.
#
# One idea recorded so nobody spends a day on it: seating the lips already
# tensioned at note-on. The valve sits at its full resting width for the first
# pass because the follower is a measurement and there is nothing yet to
# measure, so pre-tensioning looks like the fix. It is not - every e-fold by
# which the injection is reduced costs a fixed slice of time, and the note
# simply takes longer to build. Seating the tuba at 20% of its resting width
# put its excess *up* from 3.6x to 23.1x and its speak time from 70 ms to 180.
#
# The ladder is a clean gradient now and is the thing to protect: Tuba 4,
# Bass Trombone 4, Trombone 5, Horn 5, Flugel 5, Cornet 6, Trumpet 10,
# Piccolo 11, Harmon 12.
#
# **The big instruments were being blown as gently as they look**, and that
# was the last thing wrong with this bank. A column for it arrived with the
# woodwinds: `hollow` is the even harmonics against the odd ones in decibels,
# and a brass instrument's series is complete, so anything past about -15
# means the lips are barely working. The dark half of this bank read -19 to
# -28 - a tuba with its second harmonic *under* its third, which is a
# clarinet's signature and not a tuba's - because `bite`, `brass` and
# `pressure` had all been graded by the size of the instrument. They are the
# lips and the air, and a tuba takes more of both than a trumpet; what makes
# it dark is the bore and the bell, which is where `size`, `bell` and
# `cutoff` already put it. Driven properly the dark seven read -11 to -15
# with the same centroids they had, the ladder gains a harmonic or two each,
# and Section and Shambles went from 120 and 330 ms to speak down to 70 and
# 80. It cost 11 to 18 cents of pitch, which `fine` carries - the lips pull
# flat as they are leaned on, here as anywhere.
#
# `audition sweep Brazen` plays every note of every range and names the dead
# ones and the ones on the wrong partial. All 428 pass, and the tuning holds
# within 12 cents across a range except where a patch detunes itself on
# purpose (Section, Shambles, Fanfare). Straight Mute and Harmon vary 14 and
# 16 dB across a range, which is a mute's own resonance deciding how much of
# each note gets out, and is what a mute does.
#
# Levelled to about -20 dB RMS. The harmon sits 6 dB under it at its volume's
# maximum and no amount of knob will fetch it back, because most of the
# instrument is not coming out of the bell any more - which is what a mute is.
#
# Shambles still reads 330 ms to speak on a single note. Four players
# scattered over 85 ms behind a 70 ms attack is the patch, not a fault.
#
# And they are auditioned with a melody rather than one held note - `role
# tune` - because one note is what one note sounds like. Every patch in the
# bank this replaces passed a held note and they were still indistinguishable
# in a phrase.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Brazen` lists them.

machine Brazen
role    tune

patch Init family=horn

patch Tuba family=low  note=41 range=29..53
  # The widest bore and the dullest bell, blown gently. Two partials over the
  # fundamental and nothing above them.
  size           0.03
  bell           0.88
  brass          0.62
  pressure       0.88
  lipdamp        0.45
  bite           0.9
  attack         0.05 s
  release        0.32 s
  vibrato        2 cents
  # Breath right off. At the default 0.15 the noise sat *above* the tone in
  # the band brightness is measured over and the tuba read 3795 Hz - brighter
  # than the piccolo. A big instrument blown gently is nearly all fundamental
  # and the hiss has nowhere to hide behind.
  breath         0
  fine           13 cents
  volume         0.71

  cutoff         1600 Hz
patch "Bass Trombone" family=low  note=43 range=34..58
  # Still wide, but the bell lets more out than a tuba's and it is blown
  # harder, so the ladder reaches further before it gives up.
  size           0.16
  bell           0.76
  brass          0.68
  pressure       0.88
  lipdamp        0.5
  bite           0.88
  attack         0.04 s
  glide          0.09 s
  vibrato        4 cents
  breath         0.03
  fine           15 cents
  volume         0.70

  cutoff         3200 Hz
patch Trombone family=low  note=48 range=40..67
  # The slide is the glide, and it is the only thing here that has one worth
  # hearing: a trombone gets between two notes rather than stepping.
  size           0.3
  bell           0.66
  brass          0.72
  pressure       0.88
  lipdamp        0.55
  bite           0.82
  attack         0.028 s
  glide          0.12 s
  vibrato        5 cents
  breath         0.04
  fine           18 cents
  volume         0.64

  cutoff         5000 Hz
patch Horn family=horn  note=53 range=41..72
  # Bell high and the bore wide: a horn is played into its own bell and that
  # is why it is the mellow one.
  #
  # One player, and that was not the first answer. Two locked tight cancelled
  # each other on some intervals and left a hole in the middle of the phrase -
  # which is the model working, since the players really do listen to one
  # another, but a preset should not have a hole in it. Section and Shambles
  # are where the ensemble lives.
  size           0.34
  bell           0.8
  brass          0.6
  pressure       0.85
  lipdamp        0.42
  bite           0.78
  attack         0.045 s
  release        0.4 s
  vibrato        6 cents
  width          0.5
  breath         0.05
  fine           12 cents
  volume         0.62

  cutoff         4800 Hz
patch Flugel family=horn  note=58 range=52..77
  # A trumpet's pitch through a horn's bore. The one that shows size and
  # register are separate things.
  size           0.55
  bell           0.72
  brass          0.55
  pressure       0.82
  lipdamp        0.45
  bite           0.7
  attack         0.03 s
  vibrato        5 cents
  breath         0.05
  fine           7 cents
  volume         0.61

patch Cornet family=high  note=60 range=52..77
  # Between the flugel and the trumpet, which is exactly what it is.
  size           0.72
  bell           0.62
  brass          0.62
  pressure       0.85
  bite           0.72
  attack         0.022 s
  vibrato        7 cents
  breath         0.06
  fine           11 cents
  volume         0.59

patch Trumpet family=high  note=62 range=52..82
  # Narrow bore, open bell, leant on. Eight harmonics and an even rolloff -
  # the thing the tuba is not.
  size           0.95
  bell           0.58
  brass          0.78
  pressure       0.9
  lipdamp        0.62
  bite           0.6
  attack         0.014 s
  vibrato        8 cents
  vibratodelay   0.5 s
  breath         0.08
  volume         0.57

patch Piccolo family=high  note=70 range=64..91
  # The same instrument again, shorter and harder, up where it screams. The
  # top of the machine's range and the top of the bank's.
  size           1
  bell           0.64
  brass          0.9
  pressure       0.98
  lipdamp        0.7
  bite           0.75
  attack         0.008 s
  vibrato        6 cents
  breath         0.1
  fine           11 cents
  volume         0.56

patch "Straight Mute" family=muted  note=62 range=52..82
  # A mute is a filter here and not a volume knob. The straight one takes the
  # body out and leaves the edge.
  mute           #1
  mutetone       0.7
  size           0.9
  bell           0.54
  brass          0.7
  pressure       0.85
  bite           0.6
  attack         0.016 s
  breath         0.09
  volume         1.06

  cutoff         4200 Hz
patch "Cup Mute" family=muted  note=58 range=52..82
  # And the cup one takes the edge out and leaves the body: the same
  # instrument, the opposite half of it.
  mute           #2
  mutetone       0.3
  size           0.82
  bell           0.74
  brass          0.66
  pressure       0.85
  bite           0.82
  attack         0.026 s
  breath         0.06
  fine           16 cents
  volume         0.65

  cutoff         3200 Hz
patch Harmon family=muted  note=62 range=52..82
  # The one everybody knows, and the reason the mutes are filters: it is all
  # upper partials and no fundamental to speak of.
  mute           #3
  mutetone       0.65
  size           0.95
  bell           0.5
  brass          0.62
  pressure       0.86
  bite           0.55
  attack         0.02 s
  vibrato        10 cents
  breath         0.12
  volume         1.17

  cutoff         5000 Hz
patch Section family=ensemble  note=55 range=55..79
  # Four players, each a whole instrument with its own tube and its own idea
  # of the note, pulled tight together. Not one player detuned four times.
  players        #3
  lock           0.88
  spread         14 cents
  scatter        22 ms
  drift          8 cents
  # 0.85 with the lips this far into their own clamp lost six decibels
  # summed to mono: four players with four tunings are four uncorrelated
  # sources, and the wider they sit the less of them survives a phone
  # speaker. 0.7 reads +3.6 dB, which is what uncorrelated means rather
  # than what cancelling means.
  width          0.7
  size           0.55
  bell           0.6
  brass          0.6
  pressure       0.85
  bite           0.7
  attack         0.045 s
  breath         0.05
  fine           6 cents
  volume         0.43

  cutoff         4000 Hz
patch Shambles family=ensemble  note=55 range=55..79
  # The same four with nobody listening to anybody. The `lock` knob is the one
  # control here a sampled library cannot have, and this is the far end of it.
  players        #3
  lock           0.03
  spread         48 cents
  scatter        85 ms
  drift          38 cents
  width          1
  size           0.55
  bell           0.6
  brass          0.6
  pressure       0.85
  bite           0.78
  attack         0.07 s
  breath         0.06
  fine           27 cents
  volume         0.28

  cutoff         4400 Hz
patch Growl family=ensemble  note=58 range=55..77
  # The player humming against their own note: sidebands on the air and on the
  # lips at once, which is not a tremolo and does not sound like one.
  growl          0.75
  growlrate      42 Hz
  size           0.86
  bell           0.56
  brass          0.85
  pressure       0.92
  lipdamp        0.68
  bite           0.65
  drive          0.28
  attack         0.018 s
  breath         0.07
  fine           10 cents
  volume         0.86

patch Fanfare family=ensemble  note=65 range=55..79
  # Everything leant on at once. Brass at the top is the shock front, which is
  # a rolloff eight deep rather than a bag of partials - and the reason it is
  # a delay in the model and not a distortion.
  size           1
  bell           0.62
  brass          1
  pressure       0.93
  lipdamp        0.68
  bite           0.8
  attack         0.01 s
  players        #1
  lock           0.9
  spread         9 cents
  vibrato        4 cents
  breath         0.07
  fine           14 cents
  volume         0.47
